\title{Fair Admissions with Overlapping Reserve Categories under Score Constraints}
\subtitle{SPSE, PA-ODR, and the Cost of Score-Feasibility}
\author{Tomohiro Shimamura \and Zhang Yao \and Zhaohong Sun}
\institute{Kyushu University}
\date{PRIMA 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{frame}
  \titlepage

  \note{Thank you. I will talk about admissions with overlapping reserve categories under a score constraint. This is joint work with Zhang Yao and Zhaohong Sun at Kyushu University. The short version: when students are eligible for several reserve categories but count only once, deciding \emph{where} a student is counted becomes part of the allocation, and a cutoff-gap rule turns that decision into a feasibility problem.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Question}
  We study admissions to one school with:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item one open category $o$,
    \item several reserve categories $T$,
    \item students who may be eligible for multiple reserve categories,
    \item one-to-one counting: an admitted student fills at most one category,
    \item a score constraint bounding the gap between open and reserve cutoffs.
  \end{itemize}

  \vspace{0.8em}
  \begin{block}{Main issue}
    With overlapping eligibility, deciding \emph{where} a student is counted is part of the allocation problem.
  \end{block}

  \note{The setting is one school with an open category and several reserve categories. Two ingredients make it interesting. First, overlap: a student may be eligible for several reserve categories. Under one-to-one counting she fills at most one of them. Second, a score constraint: the open cutoff and each reserve cutoff may not be too far apart. This is not a toy rule. In \emph{Ashoka Kumar Thakur}, the Supreme Court of India recommended at most a ten-point gap between the open and OBC cutoffs. So the main issue is: with overlap, where a student is counted is itself a design decision.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Model}
  Each student $i$ has:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item raw score $\sigma(i)$,
    \item reserve eligibility set $\T(i)\subseteq T$,
    \item strict preference over admissible routes $V_i=\{o\}\cup\T(i)$.
  \end{itemize}

  \vspace{0.6em}
  Reserve categories use a common bonus $k$:
  \[
    \sigma_o(i)=\sigma(i),\qquad
    \sigma_t(i)=\sigma(i)+k \quad (t\in\T(i)).
  \]

  \vspace{0.6em}
  A feasible matching $\mu$ is \textbf{score-feasible} if
  \[
    e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le \delta
    \qquad \text{for every } t\in T.
  \]
  Empty reserve categories make the condition vacuous.

  \note{Each student has a raw score, a set of reserve categories she is eligible for, and a strict preference over her admissible routes: the open category plus her reserve categories. The open category ranks by raw score; every reserve category ranks by raw score plus one common bonus $k$. A matching is score-feasible if the open cutoff minus each reserve cutoff is at most $\delta$, with the reserve cutoff measured on the adjusted scale. Note the asymmetry I will keep using: the open cutoff is raw, the reserve cutoffs include the bonus. Empty categories impose nothing.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Two Fairness Notions}
  \begin{block}{Non-wastefulness}
    No unmatched student can be added to an admissible category while preserving feasibility and score-feasibility.
  \end{block}

  \vspace{0.5em}
  This is an additive notion: it keeps the existing assignment fixed.

  \vspace{1em}
  \begin{block}{Within-category fairness}
    No unmatched student with higher adjusted score can replace a lower-scoring student in the same reserve category.
  \end{block}

  \vspace{0.5em}
  This need not be weakened by the score constraint: replacing a reserve assignee by a higher-scoring eligible student only raises that reserve cutoff.

  \note{Two properties need care. Non-wastefulness must be restated: an empty reserve seat is only wasted if filling it keeps the matching score-feasible. Otherwise the seat is empty but unusable. Within-category fairness, by contrast, needs no weakening. If an unmatched student outranks someone inside a reserve category, swapping them can only raise that category's cutoff, so score-feasibility is never broken. That is our first lemma. Adding a student can lower a cutoff; replacing one by a better student cannot.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Mechanism 1: SPSE}
  \textbf{SPSE}: student-proposing deferred acceptance over categories.

  \begin{itemize}
    \item Students propose to admissible categories in preference order.
    \item Open category ranks by raw score.
    \item Reserve categories rank by adjusted score.
    \item Each student is held by at most one category.
  \end{itemize}

  \vspace{1em}
  \begin{center}
  \begin{tabular}{lc}
    \toprule
    Property & SPSE \\
    \midrule
    Feasible & Yes \\
    Non-wasteful & Yes \\
    No justified envy & Yes \\
    Strategy-proof & Yes \\
    Score-feasible & Not guaranteed \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \end{center}

  \note{The natural first mechanism is student-proposing deferred acceptance over categories, which we call SPSE. Students propose in preference order; the open category ranks by raw score, reserve categories by adjusted score. Because this is just college-admissions DA with categories as colleges, it inherits everything: feasible, non-wasteful, no justified envy, and strategy-proof. What it does not give is score-feasibility, and the next slide shows why.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{SPSE Can Violate the Score Constraint}
  \[
    T=\{t_1,t_2\},\quad q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1,\quad k=5,\quad \delta=10.
  \]

  \begin{center}
  \begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c}
    \toprule
    Student & Raw score & Eligible reserves & Preference \\
    \midrule
    $i_1$ & 100 & $\emptyset$ & $o$ \\
    $i_2$ & 95 & $\{t_1,t_2\}$ & $t_1\succ t_2\succ o$ \\
    $i_3$ & 82 & $\{t_2\}$ & $t_2\succ o$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \end{center}

  SPSE returns
  \[
    \mu_o=\{i_1\},\quad \mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\},\quad \mu_{t_2}=\{i_3\}.
  \]
  But
  \[
    e_o-e_{t_2}^+=100-(82+5)=13>10.
  \]

  The same candidates admit a score-feasible routing: count $i_2$ in $t_2$ and leave $t_1$ idle.

  \note{Three students, two reserve categories, one seat each, bonus five, gap ten. Student $i_2$ is eligible for both categories and prefers $t_1$. SPSE follows her preference, so $t_2$ goes to $i_3$, whose adjusted score is 87. The open cutoff is 100, so the gap is 13, above 10. But nothing about the candidate pool forced this. Count $i_2$ in $t_2$ instead and leave $t_1$ empty: the gap is zero. That alternative is score-feasible, non-wasteful and fair within categories, though it matches one fewer student. So the violation came from the routing.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{What Overlap Adds}
  Score-elevated policies can violate cutoff rules even without overlap: a reserve candidate may simply be too weak.

  \vspace{0.8em}
  Overlap adds a different cause:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item a student may be eligible for several reserve categories,
    \item but can protect the cutoff in only one of them,
    \item so the routing decision itself can determine score-feasibility.
  \end{itemize}

  \vspace{0.8em}
  \begin{block}{Takeaway}
    With one-to-one counting, overlapping eligibility is not bookkeeping; it is mechanism design.
  \end{block}

  \note{I want to be careful about what is new. Score-elevated policies can breach cutoff rules without any overlap: Aygün and Turhan showed this for the Indian ten-point rule. There, a reserve candidate is simply too weak. Overlap adds a second, independent cause. A strong student eligible for two categories can protect the cutoff in only one of them, because she counts once. So the routing decision itself can decide whether the rule survives. That is the part of the problem this paper is about.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Joint Feasibility Can Fail}
  Individual reserve categories may each be fillable under the score rule, yet not jointly fillable.

  \[
    T=\{t_1,t_2\},\quad q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1,\quad k=5,\quad \delta=10.
  \]
  \[
    i_1(100,\emptyset),\quad
    i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\}),\quad
    i_3(80,\{t_1\}),\quad
    i_4(78,\{t_2\}).
  \]

  Open-completeness forces $i_1$ into $o$, so reserve assignees need adjusted score at least $90$.

  Only $i_2$ clears the bar, and she counts once: each category is fillable alone, but not both at once.


  \begin{block}{Hall-type obstruction}
    Quotas are jointly fillable $\iff |N(S)|\ge\sum_{t\in S}q^t$ for every set $S$ of categories, $N(S)$ = students admissible for some $t\in S$. Singletons pass; $\{t_1,t_2\}$ fails.
  \end{block}
  {\small Cutoff-induced: without the cutoff, $i_3,i_4$ fill both. Unlike Kurata et al.\ (2017), where a straightforward extension of the disjoint-type soft-bound model may admit no stable matching.}

  \note{Next, an impossibility. Here each reserve quota can be filled on its own, score-feasibly, with the open category untouched. But not both at once. Open-completeness puts $i_1$ in the open category, so reserve assignees need adjusted score 90. Only $i_2$ clears that bar, and she counts once. In general, the quotas are jointly fillable exactly when a Hall condition holds for every set of categories. Checking categories one by one only tests singletons. The gap between the two is precisely overlap. And it is cutoff-induced: without the cutoff, $i_3$ and $i_4$ fill both. This differs from Kurata et al., where a straightforward extension of the soft-bound model may have no stable matching. For policy, the point is that quotas written category by category can silently promise more than overlap allows.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Mechanism 2: PA-ODR}
  \textbf{PA-ODR}: Preference-Aware Overlapping De-Reservation.

  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Fill the open category first by raw merit.
    \item Build a feasibility graph $G(e)$ at the induced open cutoff:
    \[
      (i,t)\in G(e)
      \iff
      t\in\T(i)\ \text{and}\ \sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta.
    \]
    \item Assign reserve seats using a lexicographic reserve-side matching.
    \item If a reserve category cannot be filled, de-reserve one unit of its capacity to the open category.
    \item Repeat until all surviving reserve capacities are filled.
  \end{enumerate}

  \note{The constructive response is PA-ODR. Fill the open category first by raw score. That fixes a cutoff $e$. Build a bipartite graph whose edges are the score-feasible student--category pairs at that cutoff. Match reserve seats on this graph. If some reserve category is not filled, move one of its seats to the open category, which lowers the cutoff, and repeat. It stops when every surviving reserve seat is filled. Score-feasibility then holds by construction, because every reserve assignee is on an edge.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{What ``Preference-Aware'' Means}
  Preferences do not determine everything.

  \vspace{0.6em}
  In PA-ODR:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item open seats are filled by raw score,
    \item de-reservation follows a fixed category order,
    \item preferences are used only on the reserve side.
  \end{itemize}

  \vspace{0.8em}
  Reserve-side matching chooses, in order:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item maximum number of reserve assignments,
    \item lexicographically best adjusted-score profile,
    \item lexicographically best preference-rank profile,
    \item fixed tie-break.
  \end{enumerate}

  Criteria 1--2 determine \emph{who} is admitted; criteria 3--4 decide \emph{where}.

  \note{``Preference-aware'' is a modest claim. The open category ignores preferences, and de-reservation follows a fixed order. Preferences enter only on the reserve side. Among matchings on the graph we first maximize size, then the adjusted-score profile, then an egalitarian preference-rank profile, then a fixed tie-break. By a matroid argument, the first two criteria already pin down \emph{who} is admitted; preferences decide only \emph{where} overlapping students are counted. The whole thing is one polynomial-time weighted matching.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Why De-Reserve?}
  PA-ODR's institutional rationale is non-wastefulness.

  \vspace{0.8em}
  If a reserve seat cannot be used without violating the cutoff rule, PA-ODR does not leave that admission capacity idle while admissible students can still enter through the open category.

  \vspace{0.8em}
  \begin{center}
  \begin{tabular}{lccc}
    \toprule
    Mechanism & Score-feasible & Keeps reserve capacity & Fills capacity \\
    \midrule
    SPSE & No & Yes & Yes \\
    Idle variant & Yes & Yes & No \\
    PA-ODR & Yes & No & Yes \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \end{center}

  PA-ODR buys score-feasibility and coverage by giving up some reserve capacity.

  \vspace{0.5em}
  {\small Intended for oversubscribed settings, $\sum_c q^c\le|I|$: otherwise it admits everyone through $o$ and retires every quota.}

  \note{Why de-reserve instead of just leaving an unfillable seat empty? The rationale is non-wastefulness. If a reserve seat cannot be used without breaking the cutoff rule, PA-ODR hands it to the open category rather than leaving admission capacity idle. The idle-seat variant is also score-feasible and keeps every quota, but fills less. So the three options trade off score-feasibility, reserve capacity, and coverage, and PA-ODR gives up reserve capacity. One caveat: PA-ODR is meant for oversubscribed schools. If total capacity exceeds the number of students, it admits everyone through the open category and retires every quota.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Properties of PA-ODR}
  \begin{center}
  \begin{tabular}{lc}
    \toprule
    Property & PA-ODR \\
    \midrule
    Terminates & Yes \\
    Output computable in polynomial time & Yes \\
    Feasible w.r.t. final capacities & Yes \\
    Score-feasible & Yes \\
    Non-wasteful w.r.t. final capacities & Yes \\
    Within-category fair & Yes \\
    \midrule
    Strategy-proof & No \\
    No justified envy & No \\
    Minimal de-reservation & No \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \end{center}

  The output depends on the order in which underfilled reserve categories are processed.

  {\small Eligibility is assumed verifiable: if self-declared, concealing it could pay.}

  \note{PA-ODR terminates, and its output can be computed in polynomial time. It is feasible and non-wasteful relative to the final capacities, score-feasible, and fair within categories, in the same unweakened sense as SPSE. It gives up strategy-proofness and no justified envy. The reason is that the open category is filled by raw score without asking students, and the egalitarian tie-break invites misreporting. It also does not minimize de-reservation. We assume eligibility is verifiable. If it were self-declared, concealing an eligibility could also pay.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Order Dependence}
  \[
    i_1(100,\emptyset),\quad i_2(84,\{t_1\}),\quad i_3(83,\{t_2\}),
  \]
  with $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$.

  Initially the open cutoff is $100$, so neither reserve candidate clears the bar.

  \vspace{0.8em}
  \begin{columns}[T]
    \begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
      \textbf{If $t_1\prec t_2$:}
      \begin{itemize}
        \item de-reserve $t_1$,
        \item $i_2$ enters open,
        \item $i_3$ can fill $t_2$,
        \item one reserve seat is lost.
      \end{itemize}
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
      \textbf{If $t_2\prec t_1$:}
      \begin{itemize}
        \item de-reserve $t_2$,
        \item $i_2$ enters open,
        \item nobody remains for $t_1$,
        \item two reserve seats are lost.
      \end{itemize}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}

  Same instance, same score rule, different surviving reserve capacity.

  {\small For \emph{any} fixed order, relabelling $t_1,t_2$ gives an instance where it is non-minimal. With few categories, minimum de-reservation can be solved directly (polynomial for bounded $|T|$).}

  \note{Here is order dependence. Initially nobody clears the bar. If we de-reserve $t_1$ first, $i_2$ moves to the open category, the cutoff drops to 84, and $i_3$ now qualifies for $t_2$. One seat lost. If we de-reserve $t_2$ first, $i_2$ is again absorbed into the open category, but she was the only student eligible for $t_1$, so $t_1$ also empties. Two seats lost. For any fixed order, relabelling the categories gives an instance where that order is non-minimal. So the order is a policy choice. With few categories, an institution can instead compute the minimum de-reservation directly; it is polynomial when the number of categories is bounded.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Prototype Check}
  Two randomized families, 100 instances each (seed 100, $\delta=8$, eligibility prob.\ $0.45$, common bonus).
  Mean $\pm$ s.d.; de-reserved = reserve \emph{seats}.

  \begin{center}
  \small
  \begin{tabular}{llccc}
    \toprule
    Family & Mechanism & Score-feasible & Matched & De-reserved seats \\
    \midrule
    $|I|=200, |T|=6$ & SPSE & 54/100 & $62.97\pm13.90$ & 0 \\
     & Idle & 100/100 & $44.57\pm11.41$ & 0 \\
     & PA-ODR & 100/100 & $62.97\pm13.90$ & $18.57/47.99$ (39\%) \\
    \midrule
    $|I|=500, |T|=8$ & SPSE & 52/100 & $187.33\pm31.17$ & 0 \\
     & Idle & 100/100 & $114.10\pm30.41$ & 0 \\
     & PA-ODR & 100/100 & $187.33\pm31.17$ & $73.24/149.82$ (49\%) \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \end{center}

  \begin{itemize}
    \item SPSE preserves capacity and coverage but may violate the score constraint.
    \item Idle categories preserve capacity and score-feasibility but lose coverage.
    \item PA-ODR preserves coverage and score-feasibility but de-reserves capacity.
  \end{itemize}

  {\small This is a sanity check, not an average-case claim.}

  \note{A small prototype check, two random families of 100 instances each. SPSE breached the score rule in about half the instances. The idle-seat variant was always score-feasible and kept every reserve seat, but matched far fewer students. PA-ODR matched exactly as many students as SPSE and was always score-feasible, but de-reserved 39 and 49 percent of reserve seats. So the trade-off is real in both directions. This is a sanity check, one seed, one parameter setting, not an average-case claim.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Main Comparison}
  \begin{center}
  \begin{tabular}{lcc}
    \toprule
    Property & SPSE & PA-ODR \\
    \midrule
    Feasibility & Yes & Yes, final capacities \\
    Non-wastefulness & Yes & Yes, final capacities \\
    Within-category fairness & Yes & Yes \\
    \midrule
    Score-feasibility & No & Yes \\
    No justified envy & Yes & No \\
    Strategy-proofness & Yes & No \\
    Reserve capacity & Fixed & De-reserved, not minimal \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \end{center}

  \vspace{0.8em}
  \begin{block}{Interpretation}
    PA-ODR is not a replacement for DA when incentives are paramount. It is a fallback rule for institutions that treat the cutoff-gap constraint as hard and also want to avoid leaving usable admission capacity idle.
  \end{block}

  \note{Putting it together. Above the line, both mechanisms satisfy the property, with PA-ODR relative to its final capacities. Below the line they differ outright. SPSE keeps capacities and incentives but can break the score rule. PA-ODR enforces the score rule at the cost of incentives, envy-freeness, and reserve capacity. So PA-ODR is not a replacement for DA when incentives matter most. It is a fallback for institutions that treat the cutoff-gap rule as hard and do not want to leave usable capacity idle.}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Takeaways}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Overlap makes category assignment substantive: a multiply eligible student can protect only one reserve cutoff.
    \item Fixed reserve capacities may be incompatible with score-feasibility, even when every category is individually fillable.
    \item SPSE inherits DA-style incentive and fairness properties, but may violate the score constraint.
    \item PA-ODR enforces score-feasibility and non-wastefulness relative to final capacities, but loses strategy-proofness, no-envy, and reserve capacity.
  \end{enumerate}

  \vspace{1em}
  \begin{block}{Open question}
    Can one obtain score-feasibility and strategy-proofness together, and what reserve-capacity cost is unavoidable? Is minimum de-reservation hard for unbounded $|T|$?
  \end{block}

  \note{To conclude. Overlap makes category assignment substantive, because a multiply eligible student protects only one cutoff. Fixed quotas can be jointly infeasible under a score rule even when each is individually fillable: a Hall-type obstruction caused by overlap. SPSE keeps the DA properties but not score-feasibility. PA-ODR restores it at a price. The main open question is whether any score-feasible mechanism can be strategy-proof, and what reserve capacity that would cost. Thank you.}
\end{frame}

